        'Defenders of stricter moderation point to real harms. Hate speech can precede violence; health misinformation may well have cost lives during the pandemic. In their view, platforms should have intervened far earlier, and the reluctance to act stemmed less from a commitment to freedom of expression than from commercial interest, since outrage generates engagement. Critics counter that moderation is applied inconsistently and without transparency. Content that one country considers legitimate political criticism may be removed at the request of another government. Deplatforming controversial figures, they warn, is a slippery slope: today\'s obvious extremist might be tomorrow\'s inconvenient journalist.',
