        'For over a century, a bronze statue of a colonial general stood in the main square of a Latin American port city. Erected in 1910 to commemorate the centenary of independence, it portrayed him on horseback, sword raised, beneath an inscription praising his "civilising mission". Generations of schoolchildren were photographed beneath it, rarely asking what that mission had involved.',
        'Researching the city\'s archives in the 1990s, a group of historians uncovered a very different story. Having examined military reports and letters written by survivors, they documented the general\'s campaigns against indigenous communities, including the destruction of entire villages. Their findings, published in academic journals, attracted little public attention at first. It was only decades later, prompted by protests in other countries, that the statue became a contested symbol.',
