      model: 'It is often said that modern societies, increasingly secular and individualistic, no longer need the rituals of the past. Traditions associated with religion, community and family obligation are seen by some as outdated, or worse, as commercialised performances with little meaning. I disagree. While some rituals have certainly lost their original purpose, many continue to meet needs that modern life has not eliminated — and in some cases has intensified. Consider the role of rituals in mourning. In my country, families gather on the Day of the Dead to remember relatives who have passed away. Many participants are not religious; some do not even believe in an afterlife. Nevertheless, they light candles, cook favourite dishes and share stories, and in doing so they give grief a shape. Without such a ritual, would people still remember their dead together? I suspect not. It is true that some celebrations have become commercialised. Christmas in many cities now begins in October and revolves around shopping, and so, increasingly, do weddings and birthdays. Yet even in these cases, the ritual core often survives: the shared meal, the gathering of family, the exchange of gifts. In conclusion, rituals evolve, but they rarely disappear. Some are passed down unchanged, others are revived or reinvented. As long as people need to mark transitions, share memories and belong to a community, they will continue to do so.',
